plit between Likely R and Lean R, with several movers toward Democrats", "Salazar's high-profile break with Trump on immigration may shore up Miami-Dade Latino support"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican edge in an R+6 seat with a well-funded incumbent, but the two live September polls show a dead heat and several raters have shifted the race toward Democrats. A modest Republican margin of about four points balances the district lean and money advantage against the tight polling and favorable headlines for Salazar's immigration positioning.", "watch": "Any further polling movement toward Rodriguez, or signs the immigration-ad strategy is backfiring with Trump-aligned voters, could tighten this into a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent María Elvira Salazar (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Representative Maria Elvira Salazar seeks another term in a Miami-Dade district that leans Republican by about six points but has grown competitive as Latino voters have shifted. She enters with a large financial advantage, roughly $2.3 million on hand compared with about $325,000 for Democrat Eliott Rodriguez, and most rating outlets still call the race for her, though several moved it toward Democrats in late September.\n\nRecent polls from Democratic-leaning sponsors show the race essentially tied, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-three chance. Salazar's ads breaking with President Trump on immigration enforcement have drawn wide attention and may help her with the district's Hispanic electorate. The forecast expects a narrow but real Republican edge, with meaningful uncertainty given thin polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2129, "margin": -4.43}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2851, "margin": -4.0}]}